{
  "content": "<updated_cheatsheet>\n- **Start by reconstructing the task spec only from user messages.** Treat assistant wording, inferred intent, and \u201chelpful\u201d reframing as non-authoritative unless later user turns explicitly confirm them. The analyzer\u2019s first job is to separate user-stated constraints from everything else.\n\n- **Treat user disclosure as incremental and time-sensitive.** After each new user shard, update the running spec by adding newly stated constraints while preserving earlier ones unless the user changes them. Re-check prior assistant work against the cumulative user-visible spec at that point in the trajectory, not against later revelations.\n\n- **Distinguish omission from contradiction.** If a constraint has not yet been stated, its absence should be treated as \u201cnot yet specified,\u201d not as an error. Flag divergence when the assistant ignores, violates, or invents beyond a constraint that is already in the user-derived spec.\n\n- **Separate \u201caligned so far\u201d from \u201cstill incomplete.\u201d** A response can be partially correct even if it does not cover the full revealed task. Preserve the useful aligned fragments in the analysis, and isolate only the missing or conflicting parts instead of collapsing the whole response into failure.\n\n- **Look for overcommitment to an early interpretation.** If the assistant locks onto one reading of the task before all user details arrive, treat that as anchoring risk and re-test whether later user turns invalidate the earlier framing. This is especially important in sharded-disclosure conversations.\n\n- **Check for specification drift introduced by the assistant.** Adding extra goals, subgoals, scope, formatting, optimization criteria, or a different problem framing is a meaningful divergence even when it sounds helpful. Prefer conservative alignment checks: helpful-sounding additions still count as drift if the user did not ask for them.\n\n- **Compare against the latest cumulative constraints, not just the latest turn.** The correct benchmark is the full user-derived spec available at that point in the conversation. A move that looked fine earlier may become misaligned once later user shards add new constraints.\n\n- **Treat the conversation state as editable when the assistant has clearly diverged.** If prior reasoning is built on a wrong assumption, prefer compacting away the mistaken context and retaining only the verified task spec plus already-aligned progress. This reduces anchoring on bad premises and lets the next turn restart cleanly.\n\n- **Use action-oriented issue statements.** Each flagged issue should clearly indicate what changed: whether the analysis should append newly disclosed constraints, discard an unsupported assumption, or replace the current state with a cleaned-up spec. Avoid vague critiques that do not tell downstream analysis what needs to be corrected.\n\n- **Prioritize contradictions and unsupported additions over stylistic differences.** The analyzer\u2019s purpose is deviation detection, not general quality review. Focus on whether the assistant answered a different task, violated an explicit requirement, or prematurely solved a subset while missing later constraints.\n\n- **Do not treat clarification-seeking as a substitute for progress in sharded settings.** Evaluate whether the assistant could continue productively with the disclosed facts instead of assuming missing details block the task. The user is a source of facts, not necessarily a conversation partner who will fill every gap.\n\n- **When judging a trajectory, preserve transferable structure in the analysis.** Record the general pattern that caused divergence\u2014constraint loss, anchoring, omission, contradiction, or spec drift\u2014so the takeaway applies across domains rather than only to the current task.\n\n- **In successful partial outcomes, identify what kept the assistant anchored to user constraints.** These stable anchors are often the most reusable positive signals: they show how the assistant stayed aligned as new user shards arrived and what should be preserved in future analyses.\n\n- **Keep the analysis compact and preserve only verified progress when editing context.** If a turn is partly correct and partly wrong, retain the correct fragments and discard the unsupported reasoning, so the next assistant step is not biased by earlier mistakes while still benefiting from genuine progress.",
